  FT["SG-H1000"] = {"id":"SG-H1000","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S1004-2024?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币（硬币面额与特征）公告 2024","en":"Currency (Denominations and Characteristics of Coins) Notification 2024","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S1004-2024","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1000-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act 1967\nCurrency\n(Denominations and Characteristics of Coins)\nNotification 2024\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act 1967, it is notified for general information that the denominations and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 1 January 2025 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1000-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE 2025 CHINESE ALMANAC COINS\n(YEAR OF THE SNAKE) TO BE ISSUED ON 1 JANUARY 2025\nPart 1\nDenomination\n$2\nMetal\n999 Fine Silver\nNickel-plated 99% Zinc\nStandard Weight\n7.776 g + 0.15 g\n20.000 g + 1.70 g\nStandard Diameter\n27.00 mm ± 0.30 mm\n40.70 mm ± 0.40 mm\nStandard Thickness\n1.90 mm ± 0.40 mm\n2.90 mm ± 0.40 mm\nShape\nRound\nEdge\nMilled\nDesign of Obverse\n1.The background of the $2 silver proof‑like colour coin and the $2 nickel‑plated zinc proof‑like coin is shiny silver.\n2.The Singapore Coat of Arms is engraved in the centre of the coin with the year‑date “2025” below it.\n3.The word “SINGAPORE” appears once in each of the 4 official languages and is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms.\nDesign of Reverse\n1.The background of the $2 silver proof‑like colour coin and the $2 nickel-plated zinc proof‑like coin is shiny silver.\n2.The $2 silver proof‑like colour coin and the $2 nickel‑plated zinc proof‑like coin share a common design which features —\n(a)a snake in the centre of the foreground;\n(b)the Henderson Waves of the Southern Ridges in the background, stretching from the left side to the right side of the coin, partially covered by the snake’s body and tail;\n(c)trees in the background, on each of the left and right sides of the coin; and\n(d)the denomination “$2” engraved and placed at 10 o’clock, to the left of the snake.\n3.The weight in troy oz and fineness of the coin are engraved above the snake of the $2 silver proof‑like colour coin.\n4.The snake featured in the $2 silver proof‑like colour coin is in colour.\n$2 Silver Proof-like Colour Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\n$2 Nickel-plated Zinc Proof-like Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\nPart 2\nDenomination\n$5\nMetal\n999 Fine Silver\n999.9 Fine Gold\nStandard Weight\n31.104 g + 0.40 g\n1.000 g + 0.05 g\nStandard Diameter/ Length and Width\n45.00 mm ± 0.40 mm\n1.Length: 15.00 mm ± 0.30 mm\n2.Width: 8.70 mm ± 0.30 mm\nStandard Thickness\n3.80 mm ± 0.70 mm\n3.30 mm ± 0.70 mm\n0.80 mm ± 0.30 mm\nShape\nOctagon\nFloral\nRectangle\nEdge\nPlain\nDesign of Obverse\n1.The background of the $5 silver proof coin and the $5 silver proof colour coin is shiny silver.\n2.The background of the $5 gold brilliant uncirculated coin is shiny gold.\n3.The Singapore Coat of Arms is engraved in the centre of the coin with the year‑date “2025” below it.\n4.The word “SINGAPORE” appears once in each of the 4 official languages and is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms.\n5.The mintmark is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms at 5 o’clock for the $5 silver proof coin and the $5 silver proof colour coin.\nDesign of Reverse\n1.The background of the $5 silver proof coin and the $5 silver proof colour coin is shiny silver.\n2.The background of the $5 gold brilliant uncirculated coin is shiny gold.\n3.The $5 silver proof coin and the $5 silver proof colour coin share a common design which features —\n(a)a snake in the centre of the foreground;\n(b)the Henderson Waves of the Southern Ridges in the background, stretching from the left side to the right side of the coin, partially covered by the snake’s body and tail;\n(c)trees in the background, on each of the left and right sides of the coin;\n(d)the denomination “$5” engraved and placed at 10 o’clock, to the left of the snake; and\n(e)the weight in troy oz and fineness of the coin, engraved above the snake.\n4.The snake featured in the $5 silver proof colour coin is in colour.\n5.The $5 gold brilliant uncirculated coin features —\n(a)a snake engraved in the centre;\n(b)the denomination “$5” engraved and placed at 6 o’clock, below the snake; and\n(c)the weight in grams and fineness of the coin, engraved above the snake.\n$5 Silver Proof Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\n$5 Silver Proof Colour Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\n$5 Gold Brilliant Uncirculated Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\nPart 3\nDenomination\n$20\nMetal\n999 Fine Silver\n999.9 Fine Gold\nStandard Weight\n155.518 g + 1.00 g\n7.776 g + 0.05 g\nStandard Diameter\n65.00 mm ± 0.80 mm\n21.96 mm ± 0.30 mm\nStandard Thickness\n9.50 mm ± 1.50 mm\n1.60 mm ± 0.40 mm\nShape\nRound\nEdge\nPlain\nMilled\nDesign of Obverse\n1.The background of the $20 silver proof coin is shiny silver.\n2.The background of the $20 gold proof coin is shiny gold.\n3.The Singapore Coat of Arms is engraved in the centre of the coin with the year‑date “2025” below it.\n4.The word “SINGAPORE” appears once in each of the 4 official languages and is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms.\n5.The mintmark is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms at 5 o’clock.\nDesign of Reverse\n1.The background of the $20 silver proof coin is shiny silver.\n2.The background of the $20 gold proof coin is shiny gold.\n3.The $20 silver proof coin and the $20 gold proof coin share a common design which features —\n(a)a snake in the centre of the foreground;\n(b)the Henderson Waves of the Southern Ridges in the background, stretching from the left side to the right side of the coin, partially covered by the snake’s body and tail;\n(c)trees in the background, on each of the left and right sides of the coin;\n(d)the denomination “$20” engraved and placed at 10 o’clock, to the left of the snake; and\n(e)the weight in troy oz and fineness of the coin, engraved above the snake.\n$20 Silver Proof Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\n$20 Gold Proof Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\nPart 4\nDenomination\n$80\nMetal\n999 Fine Silver\nStandard Weight\n1000.000 g + 5.00 g\nLength and Width\n1.Length: 114.50 mm ± 2.00 mm\n2.Width: 60.50 mm ± 2.00 mm\nStandard Thickness\n19.00 mm ± 4.00 mm\nShape\nRectangle\nEdge\nPlain\nDesign of Obverse\n1.The background of the $80 silver proof‑like coin is matt silver.\n2.The Singapore Coat of Arms is engraved in the centre of the coin with the year‑date “2025” below it.\n3.The word “SINGAPORE” appears once in each of the 4 official languages and is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms.\nDesign of Reverse\n1.The background of the $80 silver proof‑like coin is shiny silver.\n2.The $80 silver proof‑like coin features —\n(a)a snake in the centre of the foreground;\n(b)the Henderson Waves of the Southern Ridges in the background, stretching from the left side to the right side of the coin, partially covered by the snake’s body and tail and the trees on the left side of the coin;\n(c)trees in the background, on each of the left and right sides of the coin;\n(d)the denomination “$80” engraved and placed at 9 o’clock, to the left of the snake; and\n(e)the weight in kilograms and fineness of the coin, engraved above the snake.\n$80 Silver Proof-like Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\nPart 5\nDenomination\n$100\nMetal\n999.9 Fine Gold\nStandard Weight\n31.104 g + 0.10 g\nStandard Diameter\n40.00 mm ± 0.40 mm\nStandard Thickness\n2.50 mm ± 0.50 mm\nShape\nOctagon\nEdge\nPlain\nDesign of Obverse\n1.The background of the $100 gold proof coin is shiny gold.\n2.The Singapore Coat of Arms is engraved in the centre of the coin with the year‑date “2025” below it.\n3.The word “SINGAPORE” appears once in each of the 4 official languages and is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms.\n4.The mintmark is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms at 5 o’clock.\nDesign of Reverse\n1.The background of the $100 gold proof coin is shiny gold.\n2.The $100 gold proof coin features —\n(a)a snake in the centre of the foreground;\n(b)the Henderson Waves of the Southern Ridges in the background, stretching from the left side to the right side of the coin, partially covered by the snake’s body and tail;\n(c)trees in the background, on each of the left and right sides of the coin;\n(d)the denomination “$100” engraved and placed at 10 o’clock, to the left of the snake; and\n(e)the weight in troy oz and fineness of the coin, engraved above the snake.\n$100 Gold Proof Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\nPart 6\nDenomination\n$200\nMetal\n999.9 Fine Gold\nStandard Weight\n155.518 g + 0.20 g\nStandard Diameter\n60.00 mm ± 0.80 mm\nStandard Thickness\n5.50 mm ± 1.50 mm\nShape\nRound\nEdge\nPlain\nDesign of Obverse\n1.The background of the $200 gold proof coin is shiny gold.\n2.The Singapore Coat of Arms is engraved in the centre of the coin with the year‑date “2025” below it.\n3.The word “SINGAPORE” appears once in each of the 4 official languages and is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms.\n4.The mintmark is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms at 5 o’clock.\nDesign of Reverse\n1.The background of the $200 gold proof coin is shiny gold.\n2.The $200 gold proof coin features —\n(a)a snake in the centre of the foreground;\n(b)the Henderson Waves of the Southern Ridges in the background, stretching from the left side to the right side of the coin, partially covered by the snake’s body and tail;\n(c)trees in the background, on each of the left and right sides of the coin;\n(d)the denomination “$200” engraved and placed at 10 o’clock, to the left of the snake; and\n(e)the weight in troy oz and fineness of the coin, engraved above the snake.\n$200 Gold Proof Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design"}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency (Denominations and Characteristics of Coins) Notification 2024","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1001"] = {"id":"SG-H1001","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S831-2025?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币（硬币面额与特征）公告 2025","en":"Currency (Denominations and Characteristics of Coins) Notification 2025","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S831-2025","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1001-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act 1967\nCurrency\n(Denominations and Characteristics of Coins)\nNotification 2025\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act 1967, it is notified for general information that the denominations and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 1 January 2026 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1001-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE 2026 CHINESE ALMANAC COINS\n(YEAR OF THE HORSE) TO BE ISSUED ON 1 JANUARY 2026\nPart 1\nDenomination\n$2\nMetal\n999 Fine Silver\nNickel‑plated 99% Zinc\nStandard Weight\n7.776 g + 0.15 g\n20.000 g + 1.70 g\nStandard Diameter\n27.00 mm ± 0.30 mm\n40.70 mm ± 0.40 mm\nStandard Thickness\n1.90 mm ± 0.40 mm\n2.90 mm ± 0.40 mm\nShape\nRound\nEdge\nMilled\nDesign of Obverse\n1.The background of the $2 silver proof‑like colour coin and the $2 nickel‑plated zinc proof‑like coin is shiny silver.\n2.The Singapore Coat of Arms is engraved in the centre of the coin with the year‑date “2026” below it.\n3.The word “SINGAPORE” appears once in each of the 4 official languages and is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms.\nDesign of Reverse\n1.The background of the $2 silver proof‑like colour coin and the $2 nickel‑plated zinc proof‑like coin is shiny silver.\n2.The $2 silver proof‑like colour coin and the $2 nickel‑plated zinc proof‑like coin share a common design which features —\n(a)a horse in a rearing position in the centre of the foreground and on the Kelong Bridge at the Punggol Waterway, with its forelegs, head and tail partially covering the stilt‑like posts of the Kelong Bridge;\n(b)the Jewel Bridge in the background on the left side of the coin;\n(c)trees in the background on the left and right sides of the coin; and\n(d)the denomination “$2” engraved and placed at 7 o’clock, below the horse.\n3.The weight in troy oz and fineness of the coin are engraved above the horse of the $2 silver proof‑like colour coin.\n4.The horse featured in the $2 silver proof‑like colour coin is in colour.\n$2 Silver Proof‑like Colour Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\n$2 Nickel‑plated Zinc Proof‑like Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\nPart 2\nDenomination\n$5\nMetal\n999 Fine Silver\n999.9 Fine Gold\nStandard Weight\n31.104 g + 0.40 g\n1.000 g + 0.05 g\nStandard Diameter/ Length and Width\n45.00 mm ± 0.40 mm\n1.Length: 15.00 mm ± 0.30 mm\n2.Width: 8.70 mm ± 0.30 mm\nStandard Thickness\n3.80 mm ± 0.70 mm\n3.30 mm ± 0.70 mm\n0.80 mm ± 0.30 mm\nShape\nOctagon\nFloral\nRectangle\nEdge\nPlain\nDesign of Obverse\n1.The background of the $5 silver proof coin and the $5 silver proof colour coin is shiny silver.\n2.The background of the $5 gold brilliant uncirculated coin is shiny gold.\n3.The Singapore Coat of Arms is engraved in the centre of the coin with the year‑date “2026” below it.\n4.The word “SINGAPORE” appears once in each of the 4 official languages and is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms.\n5.The mintmark is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms at 5 o’clock for the $5 silver proof coin and the $5 silver proof colour coin.\nDesign of Reverse\n1.The background of the $5 silver proof coin and the $5 silver proof colour coin is shiny silver.\n2.The background of the $5 gold brilliant uncirculated coin is shiny gold.\n3.The $5 silver proof coin and the $5 silver proof colour coin share a common design which features —\n(a)a horse in a rearing position in the centre of the foreground and on the Kelong Bridge at the Punggol Waterway, with its forelegs, head and tail partially covering the stilt‑like posts of the Kelong Bridge;\n(b)the Jewel Bridge in the background on the left side of the coin;\n(c)trees in the background on the left and right sides of the coin;\n(d)the denomination “$5” engraved and placed at 7 o’clock, below the horse; and\n(e)the weight in troy oz and fineness of the coin, engraved above the horse.\n4.The horse featured in the $5 silver proof colour coin is in colour.\n5.The $5 gold brilliant uncirculated coin features —\n(a)a horse engraved in the centre;\n(b)the denomination “$5” engraved and placed at 6 o’clock, below the horse; and\n(c)the weight in grams and fineness of the coin, engraved above the horse.\n$5 Silver Proof Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\n$5 Silver Proof Colour Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\n$5 Gold Brilliant Uncirculated Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\nPart 3\nDenomination\n$20\nMetal\n999 Fine Silver\n999.9 Fine Gold\nStandard Weight\n155.518 g + 1.00 g\n7.776 g + 0.05 g\nStandard Diameter\n65.00 mm ± 0.80 mm\n21.96 mm ± 0.30 mm\nStandard Thickness\n9.50 mm ± 1.50 mm\n1.60 mm ± 0.40 mm\nShape\nRound\nEdge\nPlain\nMilled\nDesign of Obverse\n1.The background of the $20 silver proof coin is shiny silver.\n2.The background of the $20 gold proof coin is shiny gold.\n3.The Singapore Coat of Arms is engraved in the centre of the coin with the year‑date “2026” below it.\n4.The word “SINGAPORE” appears once in each of the 4 official languages and is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms.\n5.The mintmark is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms at 5 o’clock.\nDesign of Reverse\n1.The background of the $20 silver proof coin is shiny silver.\n2.The background of the $20 gold proof coin is shiny gold.\n3.The $20 silver proof coin and the $20 gold proof coin share a common design which features —\n(a)a horse in a rearing position in the centre of the foreground and on the Kelong Bridge at the Punggol Waterway, with its forelegs, head and tail partially covering the stilt‑like posts of the Kelong Bridge;\n(b)the Jewel Bridge in the background on the left side of the coin;\n(c)trees in the background on the left and right sides of the coin;\n(d)the denomination “$20” engraved and placed at 7 o’clock, below the horse; and\n(e)the weight in troy oz and fineness of the coin, engraved above the horse.\n$20 Silver Proof Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\n$20 Gold Proof Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\nPart 4\nDenomination\n$80\nMetal\n999 Fine Silver\nStandard Weight\n1000.000 g + 5.00 g\nLength and Width\n1.Length: 114.50 mm ± 2.00 mm\n2.Width: 60.50 mm ± 2.00 mm\nStandard Thickness\n19.00 mm ± 4.00 mm\nShape\nRectangle\nEdge\nPlain\nDesign of Obverse\n1.The background of the $80 silver proof‑like coin is matt silver.\n2.The Singapore Coat of Arms is engraved in the centre of the coin with the year‑date “2026” below it.\n3.The word “SINGAPORE” appears once in each of the 4 official languages and is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms.\nDesign of Reverse\n1.The background of the $80 silver proof‑like coin is shiny silver.\n2.The $80 silver proof‑like coin features —\n(a)a horse in a rearing position in the centre of the foreground and on the Kelong Bridge at the Punggol Waterway, with its forelegs, head and tail partially covering the stilt‑like posts of the Kelong Bridge;\n(b)the Jewel Bridge in the background on the left side of the coin;\n(c)trees in the background on the left and right sides of the coin;\n(d)plants in the foreground on the left and right bottom corners of the coin;\n(e)the denomination “$80” engraved and placed at 3 o’clock, to the right of the horse; and\n(f)the weight in kilograms and fineness of the coin, engraved above the horse.\n$80 Silver Proof‑like Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\nPart 5\nDenomination\n$100\nMetal\n999.9 Fine Gold\nStandard Weight\n31.104 g + 0.10 g\nStandard Diameter\n40.00 mm ± 0.40 mm\nStandard Thickness\n2.50 mm ± 0.50 mm\nShape\nOctagon\nEdge\nPlain\nDesign of Obverse\n1.The background of the $100 gold proof coin is shiny gold.\n2.The Singapore Coat of Arms is engraved in the centre of the coin with the year‑date “2026” below it.\n3.The word “SINGAPORE” appears once in each of the 4 official languages and is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms.\n4.The mintmark is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms at 5 o’clock.\nDesign of Reverse\n1.The background of the $100 gold proof coin is shiny gold.\n2.The $100 gold proof coin features —\n(a)a horse in a rearing position in the centre of the foreground and on the Kelong Bridge at the Punggol Waterway, with its forelegs, head and tail partially covering the stilt‑like posts of the Kelong Bridge;\n(b)the Jewel Bridge in the background on the left side of the coin;\n(c)trees in the background on the left and right sides of the coin;\n(d)the denomination “$100” engraved and placed at 7 o’clock, below the horse; and\n(e)the weight in troy oz and fineness of the coin, engraved above the horse.\n$100 Gold Proof Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design\nPart 6\nDenomination\n$200\nMetal\n999.9 Fine Gold\nStandard Weight\n155.518 g + 0.20 g\nStandard Diameter\n60.00 mm ± 0.80 mm\nStandard Thickness\n5.50 mm ± 1.50 mm\nShape\nRound\nEdge\nPlain\nDesign of Obverse\n1.The background of the $200 gold proof coin is shiny gold.\n2.The Singapore Coat of Arms is engraved in the centre of the coin with the year‑date “2026” below it.\n3.The word “SINGAPORE” appears once in each of the 4 official languages and is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms.\n4.The mintmark is engraved on the coin around the Singapore Coat of Arms at 5 o’clock.\nDesign of Reverse\n1.The background of the $200 gold proof coin is shiny gold.\n2.The $200 gold proof coin features —\n(a)a horse in a rearing position in the centre of the foreground and on the Kelong Bridge at the Punggol Waterway, with its forelegs, head and tail partially covering the stilt‑like posts of the Kelong Bridge;\n(b)the Jewel Bridge in the background on the left side of the coin;\n(c)trees in the background on the left and right sides of the coin;\n(d)the denomination “$200” engraved and placed at 7 o’clock, below the horse; and\n(e)the weight in troy oz and fineness of the coin, engraved above the horse.\n$200 Gold Proof Coin\nReverse Design\nObverse Design"}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency (Denominations and Characteristics of Coins) Notification 2025","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1002"] = {"id":"SG-H1002","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S494-2015?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币（纸币面额与特征）公告 2015","en":"Currency (Denominations and Characteristics of Currency Notes) Notification 2015","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S494-2015","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1002-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(CHAPTER 69)\nCurrency\n(Denominations and Characteristics of Currency Notes)\nNotification 2015\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is notified for general information that the denominations and characteristics of the currency notes issued on 11 August 2015 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1002-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE SINGAPORE50 (SG50) COMMEMORATIVE NOTES ISSUED ON 11 AUGUST 2015\nDenomination\nSize\nDesign\nPredominant and Secondary Colours\nFront\nBack\nFront\nBack\n$50\nLength — 156mm\nWidth — 74mm\nThe front design features the portrait of Singapore’s first President, the late Mr Yusof bin Ishak.\nThe large see-through window on the left, features Singapore’s first Prime Minister, the late Mr Lee Kuan Yew. There is another see-through window that is located on the bottom right-hand corner.\nBelow Mr Lee’s image is a colour-switching Singapore50 (SG50) logo and a year-date that switches from ‘1965’ to ‘2015’ when the note is tilted.\nThe image of four children of different races and gender is depicted in the centre-left portion, and the image of the Singapore Arms is located above the words ‘THIS NOTE IS LEGAL TENDER’.\nThe signature and seal of Mr Tharman Shanmugaratnam, Chairman, Monetary Authority of Singapore are found below the words ‘Fifty Dollars’ and the denomination numeral ‘50’.\nOne set of the asymmetrical serial number appears horizontal on the left-hand side while the other set appears vertical on the right-hand side.\nThe word ‘SINGAPORE’ is printed on the bottom centre-right portion. The Braille pattern is at the top right-hand corner.\nThe see-through window at the bottom right-hand corner is embossed with the denomination numeral ‘50’ in its centre, with the repetitive pattern of the denomination numeral ‘50’ at the left of that window.\nThe back design features two scenes showing the transformation of Singapore — on the left, a scene of Punggol New Town is depicted and on the centre-right, a scene of the National Day Parade in 1966 is depicted.\nThe denomination numeral ‘50’ is located at the top centre-right and at the lower centre-left of the note. The word ‘SINGAPORE’ overlaps with the denomination numeral ‘50’ at the top centre-right.\nThe 2 see-through windows located at the front of the note can also be seen from the back.\nPredominant Colour: Gold\nSecondary Colours: Brown, Blue and Red\nPredominant Colour: Gold\nSecondary Colours: Brown, Blue, Green and Red\n$10\nLength — 141mm\nWidth — 69mm\nThe front design features the portrait of Singapore’s first President, the late Mr Yusof bin Ishak.\nThere is a security feature on the left side, depicting the Singapore Arms, official SG50 logo, and year-dates ‘1965’ and ‘2015’, which have colour-switching effect.\nThe Singapore Lion symbol in gold patch with the words ‘TEN DOLLARS’ and the image of the Singapore Arms are located above the words ‘THIS NOTE IS LEGAL TENDER’. The denomination numeral ‘10’ is located at the upper-middle portion.\nThe signature and seal of Mr Tharman Shanmugaratnam, Chairman, Monetary Authority of Singapore, are below the words ‘TEN DOLLARS’.\nThe security thread in the shape of Singapore island is at the lower centre of the note.\nThe spray of the orchid Vanda Miss Joaquim, Singapore’s national flower, is located at the centre of the note.\nThe word ‘SINGAPORE’ is printed on the bottom centre portion. Another Singapore Lion symbol is located at the left side of the word ‘SINGAPORE’.\nThe Braille pattern is at the top right-hand corner. The see-through window at the bottom right corner is embossed with the denomination numeral ‘10’ in its centre, with repetitive pattern of the denomination numeral ‘10’ at the top and bottom of that window.\nBack design of the SG50 $10 Commemorative Note — ‘…regardless of race, language or religion…’\nThe back design features a group of eight students standing shoulder to shoulder.\nThe words ‘SINGAPORE Vibrant Nation, Endearing Home’ appear at the top of the note.\nThe denomination numeral ‘10’ is located at both the top left-hand corner and at the bottom centre-right of the note. The Singapore Lion symbol is located to the right of the denomination numeral ‘10’ and is in perfect registration with that on the front.\nThe see-through window located at the front of the note can also be seen from the back.\nThe words ‘…regardless of race, language or religion…’ are located at the bottom, to the right of the see-through window.\nOne set of the asymmetrical serial number appears horizontal at the bottom centre while the other set appears vertical on the right-hand side, above the Singapore Lion symbol.\nPredominant Colour: Red\nSecondary Colours: Brown and Pink-Purple\nPredominant Colour: Red\nSecondary Colour: Brown\nBack design of the SG50 $10 Commemorative Note — ‘Opportunities for All’\nThe foreground of the back design features students learning and developing in both academic and non-academic ways.\nThe left side features an image of a teacher watching a group of students play soccer, while the right side depicts a teacher holding a globe, with three students in the centre.\nThe background depicts an engineer, a dancer, a scientist, a chef and a financial analyst, portraying the various career aspirations in different sectors of the economy.\nThe words ‘SINGAPORE Vibrant Nation, Endearing Home’ appear at the top of the note.\nThe denomination numeral ‘10’ is located at the top left-hand corner and at the bottom centre-right of the note. The Singapore Lion symbol is located to the right of the denomination numeral ‘10’ and is in perfect registration with that on the front.\nThe see-through window located at the front of the note can also be seen from the back. The words ‘Opportunities for All’ are located at the bottom, to the right of the see-through window.\nOne set of the asymmetrical serial number appears horizontal at the bottom centre while the other set appears vertical on the right-hand side, above the Singapore Lion symbol.\nBack design of the SG50 $10 Commemorative Note — ‘Safe and Secure’\nThe back design features the Singapore Armed Forces, with personnel from the Navy, Army and Air Force depicted at the top of the note. This image is complemented by the image of the Home Team in the foreground, comprising Citizens-on-Patrol members, police officers, a paramedic and a firefighter.\nThe words ‘SINGAPORE Vibrant Nation, Endearing Home’ appear at the top of the note.\nThe denomination numeral ‘10’ is located at the top left-hand corner and at the bottom centre-right of the note. The Singapore Lion symbol is located to the right of the denomination numeral ‘10’ and is in perfect registration with that on the front.\nThe see-through window located at the front of the note can also be seen from the back. The words ‘Safe and Secure’ are located at the bottom, to the right of the see-through window.\nOne set of the asymmetrical serial number appears horizontal at the bottom centre while the other appears vertical on the right-hand side above the Singapore Lion symbol.\nBack design of the SG50 $10 Commemorative Note — ‘Strong Families’\nThe back design features a scene of a three-generational Chinese family celebrating the wedding anniversary for the elderly couple and a newly commissioned Indian officer sharing his achievement with his parents. The scene also portrays a Malay grandfather with his grandson and a Eurasian family riding a tandem bicycle.\nThe words ‘SINGAPORE Vibrant Nation, Endearing Home’ appear at the top of the note.\nThe denomination numeral ‘10’ is located at the top left-hand corner and at the bottom centre-right of the note. The Singapore Lion symbol is located to the right of the denomination numeral ‘10’ and is in perfect registration with that on the front.\nThe see-through window located at the front of the note can also be seen from the back. The words ‘Strong Families’ are located at the bottom, to the right of the see-through window.\nOne set of the asymmetrical serial number appears horizontal at the bottom centre while the other set appears vertical on the right-hand side, above the Singapore Lion symbol.\nBack design of the SG50 $10 Commemorative Note — ‘Caring Community, Active Citizenry’\nThe back design features a scene of community gardening, volunteers interacting with an elderly woman, and volunteers painting a flat of the less-privileged.\nThe words ‘SINGAPORE Vibrant Nation, Endearing Home’ appear at the top of the note.\nThe denomination numeral ‘10’ is located at the top left-hand corner and at the bottom centre-right of the note.\nThe Singapore Lion symbol is located to the right of the denomination numeral ‘10’ and is in perfect registration with that on the front.\nThe see-through window located at the front of the note can also be seen from the back. The words ‘Caring Community, Active Citizenry’ are located at the bottom, to the right of the see-through window.\nOne set of the asymmetrical serial number appears horizontal at the bottom centre, while the other set appears vertical on the right-hand side, above the Singapore Lion symbol.\n(a)\n(b)\n(a)Front Design of the SG50 $50 Commemorative Note.\n(b)Back Design of the SG50 $50 Commemorative Note.\nActual size: 156mm × 74mm\n(c)\n(d)\n(c)Front Design of the SG50 $10 Commemorative Note.\n(d)Back Design of the SG50 $10 Commemorative Note — ‘…regardless of race, language or religion…’.\n(e)\n(f)\n(e)Back Design of the SG50 $10 Commemorative Note — ‘Opportunities for All’.\n(f)Back Design of the SG50 $10 Commemorative Note — ‘Safe and Secure’.\n(g)\n(h)\n(g)Back Design of the SG50 $10 Commemorative Note — ‘Strong Families’.\n(h)Back Design of the SG50 $10 Commemorative Note — ‘Caring Community, Active Citizenry’.\nActual size: 141mm × 69mm"}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency (Denominations and Characteristics of Currency Notes) Notification 2015","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1003"] = {"id":"SG-H1003","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S174-1996?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":17,"structureMode":"ARTICLE","title":{"zh":"货币（全彩复印机）条例 1996","en":"Currency (Full Colour Copying Machines) Regulations 1996","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S174-1996","completenessNote":"全文，共 17 条；逐字取自新加坡法规在线（SSO）官方打印视图，未改写、未节选。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1003-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1."},"chapter":{"en":"PART I PRELIMINARY"},"heading":{"en":"Citation and commencement"},"text":{"en":"1. These Regulations may be cited as the Currency (Full Colour Copying Machines) Regulations 1996 and shall come into operation on 1st May 1996."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A2","no":2,"noLabel":{"en":"2."},"chapter":{"en":"PART I PRELIMINARY"},"heading":{"en":"Definitions"},"text":{"en":"2. In these Regulations —\n“copying machine” means a full colour copying machine that can reproduce any currency note or any design resembling a currency note;\n“permit holder” means a person who has obtained permission from the Board under section 20A(1) of the Act to import, manufacture, sell or offer for sale, or have in his possession, a copying machine."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A3","no":3,"noLabel":{"en":"3."},"chapter":{"en":"PART II USE OF COPYING MACHINE"},"heading":{"en":"Restriction on use of copying machine"},"text":{"en":"3. No person shall use a copying machine to reproduce any Singapore currency note."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A4","no":4,"noLabel":{"en":"4."},"chapter":{"en":"PART II USE OF COPYING MACHINE"},"heading":{"en":"Words, etc., to be printed behind colour copies"},"text":{"en":"4.—(1) A permit holder shall ensure that at the back of every colour copy produced by a copying machine —\n(a)the word “COPY”; and\n(b)the permit number relating to the copying machine,\nare clearly printed in such manner as the Board may require.\n(2) Paragraph (1) shall not apply if the copying machine is fitted with a counterfeit prevention system approved by the Board."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A5","no":5,"noLabel":{"en":"5."},"chapter":{"en":"PART II USE OF COPYING MACHINE"},"heading":{"en":"Register to be maintained"},"text":{"en":"5.—(1) A permit holder shall maintain a register at the place where a copying machine is kept to record all copying carried out using the machine.\n(2) The register must contain the following particulars:\n(a)the date of use;\n(b)the name, personal identification or passport number and signature of the individual or the name and signature of the officer of a company to whom copying service has been rendered;\n(c)a description of the document reproduced;\n(d)the type of material copied onto;\n(e)the name and signature of the person operating the copying machine; and\n(f)the total number of copies made.\n(3) All particulars entered in the register shall be kept for a minimum period of one year from the date of entry in the register."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A6","no":6,"noLabel":{"en":"6."},"chapter":{"en":"PART II USE OF COPYING MACHINE"},"heading":{"en":"Only authorised persons to operate copying machine"},"text":{"en":"6.—(1) A permit holder shall submit to the Board a list of persons who are authorised to operate any copying machine in his possession.\n(2) A permit holder shall ensure that any person whose name is not in the list of authorised persons will not be allowed to operate a copying machine."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A7","no":7,"noLabel":{"en":"7."},"chapter":{"en":"PART II USE OF COPYING MACHINE"},"heading":{"en":"Security of copying machine"},"text":{"en":"7. Where a copying machine is not in use, a permit holder shall ensure that the copying machine is locked and kept in a place which is secured to prevent unauthorised access to the machine."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A8","no":8,"noLabel":{"en":"8."},"chapter":{"en":"PART II USE OF COPYING MACHINE"},"heading":{"en":"Board to be informed of loss or relocation of copying machine"},"text":{"en":"8.—(1) A permit holder shall inform the Board within 7 days if a copying machine in his possession or a main component thereof is stolen or missing.\n(2) If a copying machine is removed to a place different from that previously reported to the Board, a permit holder shall give notice in writing of the removal to the Board within 7 days of the removal."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A9","no":9,"noLabel":{"en":"9."},"chapter":{"en":"PART II USE OF COPYING MACHINE"},"heading":{"en":"Board to be informed of scrapping of copying machine"},"text":{"en":"9. If a copying machine is to be disposed of by scrapping, a permit holder shall —\n(a)render the copying machine incapable of any further use; and\n(b)not later than 7 days after the scrapping, furnish to the Board a document certifying that the machine has been scrapped."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A10","no":10,"noLabel":{"en":"10."},"chapter":{"en":"PART II USE OF COPYING MACHINE"},"heading":{"en":"Board to be notified of exportation of copying machine"},"text":{"en":"10. If a copying machine is to be exported, a permit holder shall, not later than 7 days after the exportation, furnish to the Board a copy of the official export document certifying that the machine has been exported."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A11","no":11,"noLabel":{"en":"11."},"chapter":{"en":"PART II USE OF COPYING MACHINE"},"heading":{"en":"Inspection of copying machine"},"text":{"en":"11. A permit holder shall at all reasonable times permit an authorised officer of the Board to —\n(a)inspect and test a copying machine; and\n(b)inspect and make copies of particulars entered in the register maintained under regulation 5,\nand shall furnish any other relevant information the officer may require."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A12","no":12,"noLabel":{"en":"12."},"chapter":{"en":"PART III COUNTERFEIT PREVENTION SYSTEM"},"heading":{"en":"Counterfeit prevention system to be fitted"},"text":{"en":"12.—(1) No person shall, on or after 1st May 1997, be granted permission under section 20A(1) of the Act to manufacture or import a copying machine unless that machine is fitted with a counterfeit prevention system approved by the Board.\n(2) Paragraph (1) shall not apply to a copying machine that is imported for the purpose of re-export."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A13","no":13,"noLabel":{"en":"13."},"chapter":{"en":"PART III COUNTERFEIT PREVENTION SYSTEM"},"heading":{"en":"Removal of or tampering with counterfeit prevention system prohibited"},"text":{"en":"13. No permit holder shall, or shall permit a person to, remove or by any means render less effective a counterfeit prevention system approved by the Board that has been fitted in a copying machine."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A14","no":14,"noLabel":{"en":"14."},"chapter":{"en":"PART IV GENERAL PROVISIONS"},"heading":{"en":"Power of officer of Board to seal copying machine before seizure"},"text":{"en":"14. An authorised officer of the Board may seal a copying machine before seizing it under section 20A(5) of the Act."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A15","no":15,"noLabel":{"en":"15."},"chapter":{"en":"PART IV GENERAL PROVISIONS"},"heading":{"en":"Periodic reports"},"text":{"en":"15.—(1) The Board may, from time to time, require a permit holder to submit reports on such matters as the Board may require.\n(2) Such reports must be submitted within 21 days of the requisition under paragraph (1) and must be in such form as the Board may require."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A16","no":16,"noLabel":{"en":"16."},"chapter":{"en":"PART IV GENERAL PROVISIONS"},"heading":{"en":"Revocation of permit"},"text":{"en":"16. The Board may revoke the permission granted under section 20A(1) of the Act to any permit holder if he contravenes or fails to comply with any of these Regulations."}},{"id":"SG-H1003-A17","no":17,"noLabel":{"en":"17."},"chapter":{"en":"PART IV GENERAL PROVISIONS"},"heading":{"en":"Exemption"},"text":{"en":"17. The Board may exempt any person from any of the provisions of these Regulations subject to such conditions as the Board thinks fit."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1749-SG-1200","source":"Currency (Full Colour Copying Machines) Regulations 1996","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1004"] = {"id":"SG-H1004","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/Act/CA1967?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":29,"structureMode":"ARTICLE","title":{"zh":"货币法 1967","en":"Currency Act 1967","orig":null},"level":"law","status":"active","docNumber":"Act CA1967","completenessNote":"全文，共 29 条；逐字取自新加坡法规在线（SSO）官方打印视图，未改写、未节选。其中 2 条为官方正文中标注的已废止条文存根（照录）。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1004-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 1 PRELIMINARY"},"heading":{"en":"Short title"},"text":{"en":"1. This Act is the Currency Act 1967."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A2","no":2,"noLabel":{"en":"2."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 1 PRELIMINARY"},"heading":{"en":"Interpretation"},"text":{"en":"2. In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires —\n“Authority” means the Monetary Authority of Singapore established under the Monetary Authority of Singapore Act 1970;\n“currency” means currency notes and coins which are legal tender in Singapore;\n“intelligent banknote neutralisation system” or “IBNS” means a security system which is designed to deter unauthorised access to currency notes by mutilating, destroying or permanently damaging the currency notes, such as by the application of a staining or degradation agent to the currency notes or otherwise;\n“issue” includes reissue.\n[31/2017; 6/2019]"}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A3","no":3,"noLabel":{"en":"3."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 2 TRANSFER OF FUNCTIONS, PROPERTY, LIABILITIES AND EMPLOYEES TO AUTHORITY"},"heading":{"en":"Interpretation of this Part"},"text":{"en":"3. In this Part, “Board” means the Board of Commissioners of Currency, Singapore."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A4","no":4,"noLabel":{"en":"4."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 2 TRANSFER OF FUNCTIONS, PROPERTY, LIABILITIES AND EMPLOYEES TO AUTHORITY"},"heading":{"en":"Authority to administer Act"},"text":{"en":"4.—(1) The Authority is charged with the general administration of this Act and the exercise of the functions and duties imposed on the Authority by this Act.\n(2) The Authority may authorise any person to assist it in the exercise of its functions and duties under this Act, either generally or in a particular case."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A5","no":5,"noLabel":{"en":"5."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 2 TRANSFER OF FUNCTIONS, PROPERTY, LIABILITIES AND EMPLOYEES TO AUTHORITY"},"heading":{"en":"Transfer to Authority of property, assets and liabilities"},"text":{"en":"5.—(1) As from 1 October 2002, such movable and immovable property vested in the Board as may be determined by the Minister and all assets, interests, rights, privileges, liabilities and obligations of the Board shall be transferred to and shall vest in the Authority without further assurance, act or deed.\n(2) If any question arises as to whether any particular property, asset, interest, right, privilege, liability or obligation has been transferred to or vested in the Authority under subsection (1), a certificate under the hand of the Minister is conclusive evidence that the property, asset, interest, right, privilege, liability or obligation was or was not so transferred or vested.\n(3) Any immovable property to be transferred to and vested in the Authority under subsection (1) is to be held by the Authority upon such tenure and subject to such terms and conditions as the President may determine.\n(4) Every proceedings by or against the Board which are pending on 1 October 2002 may be continued, completed and enforced by or against the Authority.\n(5) Every agreement relating to any of the transferred properties to which the Board was a party immediately before 1 October 2002, whether or not of such nature that the rights and liabilities thereunder could be assigned, has effect as from that date as if —\n(a)the Authority had been a party to such an agreement; and\n(b)for any reference to the Board there were substituted in respect of anything to be done on or after 1 October 2002 a reference to the Authority.\n(6) To avoid doubt, it is declared that —\n(a)any reference in this Part to property vested in the Board is a reference to such property of the Board whether situated in Singapore or elsewhere; and\n(b)any such reference to rights and liabilities of the Board is a reference to such rights to which the Board is entitled or (as the case may be) such liabilities to which the Board is subject, whether under the laws of Singapore or any country outside Singapore and includes rights and liabilities arising under loans raised by the Board."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A6","no":6,"noLabel":{"en":"6."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 2 TRANSFER OF FUNCTIONS, PROPERTY, LIABILITIES AND EMPLOYEES TO AUTHORITY"},"heading":{"en":"Transfer of employees"},"text":{"en":"6.—(1) As from 1 October 2002, all persons employed immediately before that date by the Board shall be transferred to the service of the Authority on terms no less favourable than those enjoyed by them immediately prior to their transfer.\n(2) Until such time as terms and conditions of service are drawn up by the Authority, the scheme and terms and conditions of service in the Board continue to apply to every person transferred to the service of the Authority under subsection (1) as if the person were still in the employment of the Board."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A7","no":7,"noLabel":{"en":"7."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 2 TRANSFER OF FUNCTIONS, PROPERTY, LIABILITIES AND EMPLOYEES TO AUTHORITY"},"heading":{"en":"Service rights, etc., of transferred employees to be preserved"},"text":{"en":"7.—(1) The terms and conditions to be drawn up by the Authority must take into account the salaries and terms and conditions of service, including any accrued rights to leave, enjoyed by the persons transferred to the service of the Authority under section 6 while in the employment of the Board.\n(2) Any term or condition relating to the length of service with the Authority must provide for the recognition of service under the Board by the persons transferred under section 6 to be service by them under the Authority."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A8","no":8,"noLabel":{"en":"8."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 2 TRANSFER OF FUNCTIONS, PROPERTY, LIABILITIES AND EMPLOYEES TO AUTHORITY"},"heading":{"en":"Existing contracts"},"text":{"en":"8. All deeds, bonds, agreements, instruments and arrangements subsisting immediately before 1 October 2002 to which the Board is a party continue in force on and after that date and are enforceable by or against the Authority as if the Authority had been named therein or had been a party thereto instead of the Board."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A9","no":9,"noLabel":{"en":"9."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 2 TRANSFER OF FUNCTIONS, PROPERTY, LIABILITIES AND EMPLOYEES TO AUTHORITY"},"heading":{"en":"Continuation and completion of disciplinary proceedings"},"text":{"en":"9.—(1) Where on 1 October 2002 any disciplinary proceedings were pending against any employee of the Board transferred to the service of the Authority under section 6, the proceedings must be carried on and completed by the Authority.\n(2) Where on 1 October 2002 any matter was in the course of being heard or investigated or had been heard or investigated by a committee acting under due authority but no order, ruling or direction had been made on the matter, the committee must —\n(a)complete the hearing or investigation; and\n(b)make such order, ruling or direction as it could have made under the authority vested in it before that date.\n(3) Any order, ruling or direction made by a committee under this section is to be treated as an order, a ruling or a direction of the Authority and has the same force or effect as if it had been made by the Authority pursuant to the authority vested in the Authority under this Act."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A10","no":10,"noLabel":{"en":"10."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 2 TRANSFER OF FUNCTIONS, PROPERTY, LIABILITIES AND EMPLOYEES TO AUTHORITY"},"heading":{"en":"Misconduct or neglect of duty by employee before transfer"},"text":{"en":"10. The Authority may reprimand, reduce in rank, retire, dismiss or punish in some other manner a person who had, while the person was in the employment of the Board, been guilty of any misconduct or neglect of duty which would have rendered the person liable to be reprimanded, reduced in rank, retired, dismissed or punished in some other manner if the person had continued to be in the employment of the Board and if this Act had not been enacted."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A11","no":11,"noLabel":{"en":"11."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 3 CURRENCY"},"heading":{"en":"Currency of Singapore"},"text":{"en":"11.—(1) The unit of currency of Singapore is the Singapore dollar, which is divided into 100 cents.\n(2) The abbreviated form of the Singapore dollar is “S$” or “SGD”."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A12","no":12,"noLabel":{"en":"12."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 3 CURRENCY"},"heading":{"en":"Use of Singapore dollar"},"text":{"en":"12. All monetary obligations or transactions in Singapore are deemed to be expressed and recorded, and must be settled in the Singapore dollar unless otherwise provided for by law or validly agreed upon between the parties."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A13","no":13,"noLabel":{"en":"13."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 3 CURRENCY"},"heading":{"en":"Authority to have sole right to issue currency; legal tender"},"text":{"en":"13.—(1) The Authority has the sole right to issue currency notes and coins in Singapore and only such notes and coins issued by the Authority are legal tender in Singapore.\n(2) Currency notes issued by the Authority, if the notes have not been illegally dealt with, are legal tender up to their face value for the payment of any amount.\n(3) Coins of a denomination issued by the Authority, if the coins have not been illegally dealt with, are legal tender up to their face value for the payment of an amount not exceeding 20 times the face value of a coin of that denomination.\n[6/2019]\n(4) Where a payee has given written notice to the payer that the payee would not accept as payment all or any of the denominations of currency notes or coins in satisfaction of a debt, subsection (2) or (3) (as the case may be) does not apply to the payment of the debt to the extent specified in the notice.\n(5) Where no written notice under subsection (4) has been given by the payee, payment by the payer in accordance with subsection (2) or (3) (as the case may be) in satisfaction of a debt, is deemed to have satisfied that debt.\n(6) For the purposes of this Act —\n(a)a coin is deemed to have been illegally dealt with where the coin has been impaired, diminished, or lightened otherwise than by fair wear and tear, or has been defaced by having any name, word, device or number stamped or engraved on the coin, whether the coin has or has not been diminished or lightened by such defacement; and\n(b)a currency note is deemed to have been illegally dealt with where the note has been impaired, diminished or affected otherwise than by fair wear and tear, or has been defaced by writing or impressing on any note any mark, word, letter or figure or by perforation, cutting, splitting or in any other manner, whether the note has or has not been impaired or diminished by such defacement.\n(7) For the purposes of this Act, a currency note is also deemed to have been illegally dealt with if the currency note has been mutilated, destroyed or permanently damaged (whether by the application of a staining or degradation agent to the currency notes or otherwise) as a result of the activation of an IBNS.\n[6/2019]\n(8) In any criminal proceedings in which the genuineness of any currency note or coin may be in question, a certificate signed by an officer of the Authority authorised for that purpose that the officer is satisfied by personal examination that such note or coin is or is not forged shall be held to be conclusive evidence of the same.\n(9) An officer of the Authority must not be cross‑examined with regard to the contents of such certificate unless the court otherwise orders."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A14","no":14,"noLabel":{"en":"14."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 3 CURRENCY"},"heading":{"en":"Bills and notes payable to bearer on demand"},"text":{"en":"14.—(1) No person, except with the permission of the Authority, may —\n(a)draw, accept, make or issue any bill of exchange, promissory note or engagement for the payment of money payable to bearer on demand; or\n(b)borrow, owe or take up any sum or sums of money on bills or notes payable to bearer on demand.\n(2) Cheques or drafts payable to bearer on demand may be drawn on bankers or agents by their customers or constituents in respect of moneys in the hands of these bankers or agents held by them at the disposal of the persons drawing such cheques or drafts.\n(3) A person who contravenes this section shall, despite anything to the contrary in the Criminal Procedure Code 2010, be liable on conviction by a Magistrate’s Court to a fine equal to the amount of the bill, note or engagement in respect of which the offence is committed even if the amount of such fine may be in excess of the original jurisdiction of such Court."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A15","no":15,"noLabel":{"en":"15."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 3 CURRENCY"},"heading":{"en":"Exchange of currency notes and coins"},"text":{"en":"15.—(1) The Authority is to arrange for the reissue and exchange of currency notes and coins subject to such conditions as may be prescribed.\n(2) Where the Authority transfers its functions in relation to the reissue and exchange of currency notes and coins to another body, that body may charge for its services in respect thereof.\n(3) The Authority may charge any person or class of persons such fee as may be prescribed for the reissue and exchange of currency notes or coins with the Authority under this section."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A16","no":16,"noLabel":{"en":"16."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 3 CURRENCY"},"text":{"en":"16. [Repealed by Act 31 of 2017]"},"status":"repealed"},{"id":"SG-H1004-A17","no":17,"noLabel":{"en":"17."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 3 CURRENCY"},"heading":{"en":"Form and design of currency notes and coins"},"text":{"en":"17.—(1) Currency notes issued by the Authority must be of such denomination and of such form and design and printed from such plates and on such material as the Authority may, from time to time, decide.\n(2) The dies, plates and other instruments or materials used for the printing of notes and the currency notes printed must be prepared and kept, issued and destroyed in accordance with any direction of the Authority.\n(3) Coins issued by the Authority must be of such denominations and of such weight, form and design, and made of such materials as the Authority may, from time to time, decide.\n(4) The dies and other instruments or materials used for the minting of coins and the currency coins minted must be prepared and kept, issued and destroyed in accordance with any direction of the Authority.\n(5) The Authority must publish in the Gazette the denominations and other characteristics of currency notes and coins issued by it."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A18","no":18,"noLabel":{"en":"18."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 3 CURRENCY"},"heading":{"en":"Demonetisation of currency notes and coins"},"text":{"en":"18.—(1) The Authority may, by notification in the Gazette, withdraw any particular issue or denomination of currency notes and coins issued by the Authority which upon such withdrawal cease to be legal tender.\n(2) Any such notification must give holders of the currency notes and coins to be withdrawn a reasonable period, in any event at least 6 months, within which such notes or coins may be exchanged at their face value for other legal tender issued by the Authority."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A19","no":19,"noLabel":{"en":"19."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 3 CURRENCY"},"heading":{"en":"Mutilated currency notes and coins"},"text":{"en":"19.—(1) A person is not entitled to recover from the Authority the value of any mutilated currency note or coin or any note or coin which has been illegally dealt with.\n(2) The circumstances in which, and the conditions and limitations subject to which the value of mutilated currency notes or coins, or notes or coins which have been illegally dealt with, may be refunded as an act of grace are in the absolute discretion of the Authority.\n(3) Mutilated currency notes or coins, or currency notes or coins that have been illegally dealt with, must be repossessed by any officer of the Authority or other person authorised by the Authority when tendered to the Authority or such person, as the case may be."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A20","no":20,"noLabel":{"en":"20."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 3 CURRENCY"},"heading":{"en":"Restriction on use of photographs, drawing or design of currency notes and coins in advertisements, etc."},"text":{"en":"20.—(1) Except with the permission of the Authority, a person must not —\n(a)in any size, scale or colour, use any photograph of or any drawing or design resembling any currency note or coin or part thereof, in any advertisement; or\n(b)import, manufacture, sell, circulate or otherwise distribute any merchandise or product containing such a photograph, drawing or design in any size, scale or colour.\n(2) Except with the permission of the Authority, a person must not import, manufacture, sell, circulate or otherwise distribute any merchandise or product containing any design or part thereof used in or on any currency note or coin.\n(3) The Authority may, in granting any permission under subsection (1) or (2), impose such conditions as it thinks fit.\n(4) A person must not make or issue a piece of gold, silver, copper, nickel, bronze or of any other material whether metal or otherwise, of any value, resembling or similar to any coin issued under this Act or any other written law relating to the issue of currency which was in force at any time before 30 April 1982.\n(5) A person who contravenes this section shall be guilty of an offence and shall be liable on conviction to a fine not exceeding $2,000 or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding 3 months or to both."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A21","no":21,"noLabel":{"en":"21."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 3 CURRENCY"},"heading":{"en":"Dissolution of Currency Fund"},"text":{"en":"21. The Currency Fund established under section 21 in force immediately before 15 August 2017 is dissolved on the date of the completion of the transfer of all of its assets and liabilities to the accounts holding the Authority’s assets and liabilities, as set out in the notification mentioned in section 6A(2) of the Monetary Authority of Singapore Act 1970.\n[31/2017]"}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A22","no":22,"noLabel":{"en":"22."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 3 CURRENCY"},"heading":{"en":"Authority’s assets must not be less than currency in circulation"},"text":{"en":"22.—(1) The gross assets of the Authority must at all times be not less than 100% of the face value of the Authority’s currency in circulation.\n[31/2017]\n(2) If, at any time, the face value of the Authority’s currency in circulation exceeds the gross assets of the Authority, the amount of the deficiency is to be charged on and paid out of the Consolidated Fund.\n[31/2017]"}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A23","no":23,"noLabel":{"en":"23."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 4 MISCELLANEOUS"},"heading":{"en":"Mutilating, destroying or defacing currency notes and coins"},"text":{"en":"23.—(1) Subject to subsections (2) and (3), any person who —\n(a)mutilates or destroys any currency note or coin;\n(b)causes any change in a coin so as to destroy or diminish its value or utility;\n(c)prints or stamps, or by any similar means writes, or impresses, on any currency note any mark, word, letter or figure; or\n(d)defaces any coin by stamping thereon any name or word, whether the coin is or is not thereby impaired, diminished or lightened,\nshall be guilty of an offence and shall be liable on conviction to a fine not exceeding $2,000.\n[6/2019]\n(2) To avoid doubt, a licensed security service provider who sells any IBNS or who employs an IBNS in carrying out cash‑in‑transit services is not, by reason only of such sale or employment, liable to be convicted of an offence under —\n(a)subsection (1)(a) for any mutilation or destruction of a currency note; or\n(b)subsection (1)(c) for any printing or stamping, or by any similar means any writing or impressing, of any mark, word, letter or figure on a currency note,\nthat is caused by the activation of the IBNS.\n[6/2019]\n(3) An applicant for a security service provider’s licence to sell any IBNS, or to employ an IBNS in carrying out cash‑in‑transit services, is not liable to be convicted of an offence under —\n(a)subsection (1)(a) for any mutilation or destruction of a currency note; or\n(b)subsection (1)(c) for any printing or stamping, or by any similar means any writing or impressing, of any mark, word, letter or figure on a currency note,\nthat is caused by the activation of the IBNS in any trial or demonstration of the applicant’s IBNS that is required by a licensing officer for the purpose of deciding the application.\n[6/2019]\n(4) This section is not to be construed as limiting or affecting the provisions of the Penal Code 1871.\n(5) In this section —\n“licensed security service provider” means a holder of a security service provider’s licence under the Private Security Industry Act 2007 —\n(a)to sell any IBNS; or\n(b)to employ an IBNS in carrying out cash‑in‑transit services;\n“licensing officer” means a licensing officer as defined in section 2 of the Private Security Industry Act 2007;\n“security service provider’s licence” means a security service provider’s licence granted under the Private Security Industry Act 2007.\n[6/2019]"}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A24","no":24,"noLabel":{"en":"24."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 4 MISCELLANEOUS"},"heading":{"en":"Counterfeit currency notes and coins to be forfeited"},"text":{"en":"24. An officer of the Authority is empowered to seize any currency note or coin tendered to the Authority, which the officer believes on reasonable grounds to be counterfeit and, upon such seizure, that note or coin is forfeited to the Authority."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A25","no":25,"noLabel":{"en":"25."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 4 MISCELLANEOUS"},"heading":{"en":"Power of arrest"},"text":{"en":"25. Any police officer may arrest without warrant any person —\n(a)offending in the police officer’s view against section 14, 20 or 23; or\n(b)against whom a reasonable complaint has been made or creditable information has been received or a reasonable suspicion exists of the person having contravened section 14, 20 or 23."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A26","no":26,"noLabel":{"en":"26."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 4 MISCELLANEOUS"},"heading":{"en":"Consent of Public Prosecutor"},"text":{"en":"26. No prosecution for any offence under this Act may be instituted without the consent of the Public Prosecutor.\n[15/2010]"}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A27","no":27,"noLabel":{"en":"27."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 4 MISCELLANEOUS"},"text":{"en":"27. [Repealed by Act 24 of 2003]"},"status":"repealed"},{"id":"SG-H1004-A28","no":28,"noLabel":{"en":"28."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 4 MISCELLANEOUS"},"heading":{"en":"Regulations"},"text":{"en":"28. The Authority may make such regulations as may be required from time to time for carrying into effect the provisions of this Act or prescribing anything that may be prescribed."}},{"id":"SG-H1004-A29","no":29,"noLabel":{"en":"29."},"chapter":{"en":"PART 4 MISCELLANEOUS"},"heading":{"en":"Saving and transitional provisions"},"text":{"en":"29.—(1) On 1 October 2002 —\n(a)the Board of Commissioners of Currency, Singapore (called in this section the Board) shall be dissolved;\n(b)currency notes and coins issued by the Board before 1 October 2002 shall for all purposes be deemed to have been issued by the Authority;\n(c)the reserves accumulated by the Board before the current term of office of the Government shall be added to the reserves accumulated by the Authority before such term; and\n(d)all assets and moneys of the Currency Reserve Fund shall be transferred to the Currency Fund.\n(2) Any decision made by the Board under this Act before 1 October 2002 is deemed to be a decision made by the Authority.\n(3) Any permission, consent, approval or authorisation granted by the Board under this Act before 1 October 2002 is, so far as it is not inconsistent with the provisions of this Act, deemed to be a permission, consent, approval or authorisation granted by the Authority.\n(4) Any certificate, notice, order, direction or declaration issued or made by the Board or an officer of the Board under this Act before 1 October 2002 is deemed to be a certificate, notice, order, direction or declaration issued or made by the Authority or an officer of the Authority.\n(5) Where in any document any reference is made to the Board of Commissioners of Currency, Singapore, such reference is, as from 1 October 2002, to be read as the Monetary Authority of Singapore."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1749-SG-1200","source":"Currency Act 1967","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1005"] = {"id":"SG-H1005","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S539-2002?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act – Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S539-2002","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1005-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denominations and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 12th October 2002 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore, are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1005-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE ESPLANADE — THEATRES ON THE BAY $5 COMMEMORATIVE COINS\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandard Weight\nStandard Diameter\nStandard Thickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n5 Dollars\n999 Fine Silver\n20.00 g\n+ 0.20 g\n38.70 mm\n+ 0.15 mm\n2.30 mm\n+ 0.20 mm\nRound\nMilled and bearing the words “MONETARY AUTHORITY OF SINGAPORE” and lion symbol.\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2002” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin. The position of the mintmark is at 5 o’ clock of the coin.\nQuadrant 1\nThe upper left quadrant of the design shows the stylized architecture of the Esplanade, which is unique to Singapore.\n5 Dollars\n75% Copper\n25% Nickel\n20.00 g\n+ 0.40 g\n38.70 mm\n+ 0.125 mm\n2.45 mm\n+ 0.152 mm\nRound\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2002” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin.\nQuadrant 2\nThe upper right quadrant is a reproduction of the Esplanade logo signifying the Esplanade as a centre for performing arts from all over the world. It also signifies that the Esplanade is accessible to everyone.\nQuadrant 3\nThe lower left quadrant shows a section of the globe, symbolizing the Esplanade’s aspiration to be internationally recognised for its creativity and sense of adventure.\nQuadrant 4\nThe bottom right quadrant depicts a pair of Asian dancers in graceful harmony while the ribbon, fan and kompang inject vibrancy into their movements. The whole design in uniquely Singaporean.\n(a) Reverse Design of $5 Silver Proof and $5 Uncirculated Cupro-Nickel Coins.\n(b) Obverse Design of $5 Silver Proof Coin.\n(c) Obverse Design of $5 Uncirculated Cupro-Nickel Coin."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act – Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1006"] = {"id":"SG-H1006","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S628-2002?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——2003 年 1 月 3 日发行的硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act – Denominations and Characteristics of Coins to Be Issued on 3-1-2003","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S628-2002","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1006-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denominations and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 3rd January 2003 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore, are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1006-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE 2003 CHINESE ALMANAC COINS (YEAR OF THE GOAT)\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandard Weight\nStandard Diameter\nStandard Thickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n500 Dollars\n999.9 Fine Gold\n155.52 g\n+ 0.20 g\n55.00 mm\n+ 0.30 mm\n4.40 mm\n+ 0.30 mm\nRound\nMilled and bearing the words “MONETARY AUTHORITY OF SINGAPORE” and lion symbol.\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2003” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin. The position of the mintmark is at 5 o’ clock for all the coins except for the $10 cupronickel and $1 gold coins.\nStylized version of a goat design is to the centre. The coin design is based on the paper-cut-style. The 2 characters signifying the “Year of Goat” are above the goat design. The denomination of the coin in figures is below the design. The troy weight and fineness of the coin are at the lower circumference for all the coins except for the $10 cupro-nickel coin.\n250 Dollars\n999.9 Fine Gold\n31.103 g\n+ 0.05 g\n32.12 mm\n+ 0.15 mm\n2.50 mm\n+ 0.20 mm\nRound\n25 Dollars\n999 Fine Silver\n155.52 g minimum\n65.00 mm\n+ 0.30 mm\n5.40 mm\n+ 0.30 mm\nRound\n10 Dollars\n999 Fine Silver\n62.206 g\n+ 0.20 g\n40.70 mm\n+ 0.15 mm\n5.80 mm\n+ 0.20 mm\nRound\n10 Dollars\n75% Copper\n25% Nickel\n28.00 g\n+ 0.40 g\n40.70 mm\n+ 0.15 mm\n3.05 mm\n+ 0.20 mm\nRound\n5 Dollars\n999.9 Fine Gold\n7.776 g\n+ 0.05 g\n21.96 mm\n+ 0.125 mm\n1.55 mm\n+ 0.20 mm\nRound\n2 Dollars\n999 Fine Silver\n20.00 g 0.20 g\n38.70 mm\n+ 0.15 mm\n2.45 mm\n+ 0.30 mm\nRound\n1 Dollar\n999.9 Fine Gold\n0.30 g + 0.04 g\n7.00 mm\n+ 0.15 mm\n0.70 mm\n+ 0.15 mm\nRound\nPlain\n(a) Reverse Design of the $500 Gold Proof Coin.\n(b) Reverse Design of the $250 Gold Proof Coin.\n(c) Reverse Design of the $5 Gold Proof Coin.\n(d) Reverse Design of the $1 Gold (Brilliant Uncirculated) Coin.\n(e) Reverse Design of the $25 Silver Proof Coin.\n(f) Reverse Design of the $10 Silver Piedfort Proof Coin.\n(g) Reverse Design of the $2 Silver Proof Coin.\n(h) Reverse Design of the $10 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin.\n(i) Common Obverse Design for $500 Gold Proof Coin, $250 Gold Proof Coin, $25 Silver Proof Coin, $10 Silver Piedfort Proof Coin, $5 Gold Proof Coin and $2 Silver Proof Coin.\n(j) Common Obverse Design for $10 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin and $1 Gold (Brilliant Uncirculated) Coin."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act – Denominations and Characteristics of Coins to Be Issued on 3-1-2003","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1007"] = {"id":"SG-H1007","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S345-2003?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Coin","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S345-2003","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1007-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that denomination and characteristics of the coin to be issued on 22nd July 2003 by the Monetary Authority Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1007-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE TRIBUTE TO OUR HEALTHCARE GIVERS $2 COMMEMORATIVE COINS\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandardWeight\nStandard Diameter\nStandard Thickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n2 Dollars\n999 Fine Silver\n20.00 g + 0.20 g\n38.70 mm + 0.15 mm\n2.30 mm + 0.20 mm\nRound\nMilled and Bearing the words “MONETARY AUTHORITY OF SINGAPORE” and lion symbol.\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2003” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin. The position of the mintmark is at 5 o’clock of the coin.\nHealthcare givers in Singapore featured with the use of stylised symbols of a healthcare attendant, surgeon, doctor, virologist nurse. In the background is the Ministry of Health Building, which symbolises the leadership of the Ministry in this health crisis. The denomination “$2” is on the right-hand side of the building. Embracing the design are the words\n“TRIBUTE TO OUR HEALTH CARE GIVERS” — an expression of the respect and admiration of all Singaporeans for this group of heroes in combating Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS).\n(a) Obverse Design of $2 Silver Proof Coin.\n(b) Reverse Design of $2 Silver Proof Coin.\n(c) Obverse Design of $2 Uncirculated Cupro-Nickel Proof-like Coin.\n(d) Reverse Design of $2 Uncirculated Cupro-Nickel Proof-like Coin."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Coin","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1008"] = {"id":"SG-H1008","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S504-2004?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Coin","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S504-2004","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1008-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\nChapter 69\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denomination and characteristics of the coin to be issued on 7th September 2004 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1008-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE BRUNEI ROYAL WEDDING $10000 COMMEMORATIVE COIN TO BE ISSUED ON 7TH SEPTEMBER 2004\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandard Weight\nStandard Diameter\nStandard Thickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n10000 Dollars\n999.9 Fine Gold\n373.24 g + 3.00 g\n65.00 mm ± 0.30 mm\n6.50 mm ± 0.50 mm\nRound\nMilled and bearing the words “MONETARY AUTHORITY OF SINGAPORE” and lion symbol.\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the “$10000” denomination above it and the year-date “2004” below it. The word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin. The position of the mintmark is at 5 o’ clock.\nThe main feature of the coin is the portrait of the royal couple in the centre, with the words “DYTM PADUKA SERI PENGIRAN MUDA MAHKOTA PENGIRAN MUDA HAJI AL-MUHTADEE BILLAH AND DAYANGKU SARAH BINTI PENGIRAN SALLEH AB RAHAMAN” below it. At the circumference, the words “IN COMMEMORATION OF THE ROYAL WEDDING OF THE CROWN PRINCE OF BRUNEI DARUSSALAM” are above the portrait, and the wedding date “9 SEPTEMBER 2004” at the bottom.\n(a)Reverse Design of the $10000 Gold Proof Coin.\n(b)Obverse Design of the $10000 Gold Proof Coin."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Coin","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1009"] = {"id":"SG-H1009","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S171-2007?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Coins","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S171-2007","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1009-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denomination and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 30th April 2007 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1009-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE 2007 SINGAPORE IDENTITY PLAN (RUSTIC COAST) COINS TO BE ISSUED ON 30TH APRIL 2007\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandard Weight\nStandard\nDiameter\nStandard\nThickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n1 Dollar\n999 Fine Silver\n17.30 g 0.20 g\n33.00 mm 0.15 mm\n2.45 mm 0.30 mm\nRound\nMilled\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2007” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin. The position of the mintmark is at 5 o’clock of the coin.\nRustic Coast comprises Changi Village, Pasir Ris Park, Pulau Ubin and Punggol Point & Coney Island which are located in the north-east coast of Singapore. It boasts of a cluster of sandy beaches, idyllic islands, lush greenery and scenic waterfronts that provide relief from the bustle of the city.\nChangi Village\nChangi Village remains a popular haunt for locals and foreign visitors alike with its cozy mix of shops and eateries and the Changi Beach Park, a stone’s throw away. The coin features the distinctively recognisable 1.2 km coastline boardwalk that connects Changi Beach Club with Changi Sailing Club. The new Changi Point Ferry Terminal in the background enhances the special identity of the Village.\nPasir Ris Park\nWhat used to be a laid-back rural area with its kampongs and villages, Pasir Ris Park is now a popular weekend destination for families with its sandy white beaches, wide bicycle track and an interesting children’s playground. The coin features the iconic giant Space-Net, a perennial favourite with kids, and the various recreational activities that are a familiar sight at the Park.\nPulau Ubin\nPulau Ubin is one of the last few rustic, idyllic places where the city-weary can retreat to and marvel at the beauty of nature and the diverse marine fauna and flora. Depicted on the coin are the uniquely designed jetty and the characteristic bumboat. Adding a touch of serenity is the sprawling mangrove swamps and kampong houses on stilts.\nPunggol Point & Coney Island\nPreviously well known for its boatels and seafood restaurants, Punggol Point is now set in the peaceful surroundings of residential homes, holiday camps, beaches, a marina and a jetty. Depicted on the coin is a typical scene at Punggol Point, particularly on weekends with people engaging in water recreational activities like windsurfing, canoeing and fishing, or simply relaxing at the jetty. In the background is Coney Island, a little-known treasure trove of beautiful sandy beaches and tall Casuarina trees.\nThe denomination of the coin in figures is to the right of the main design on all the coins.\n1 Dollar\n75% Copper\n25% Nickel\n18.00 g 0.30 g\n33.00 mm 0.15 mm\n2.80 mm 0.30 mm\nRound\nMilled\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2007” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin.\n(a)Reverse Design of the $1 Silver Proof Coin (Changi Village).\n(b) Reverse Design of the $1 Silver Proof Coin (Pasir Ris Park).\n(c) Reverse Design of the $1 Silver Proof Coin (Pulau Ubin).\n(d) Reverse Design of the $1 Silver Proof Coin (Punggol Point & Coney Island).\n(e) Reverse Design of the $1 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin (Changi Village).\n(f) Reverse Design of the $1 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin (Pasir Ris Park).\n(g)Reverse Design of the $1 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin (Pulau Ubin).\n(h)Reverse Design of the $1 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin (Punggol Point & Coney Island).\n(i)Common Obverse Design for $1 Silver Proof Coins (Changi Village, Pasir Ris Park, Pulau Ubin and Punggol Point & Coney Island).\n(j)Common Obverse Design for $1 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coins (Changi Village, Pasir Ris Park, Pulau Ubin and Punggol Point & Coney Island)."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Coins","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1010"] = {"id":"SG-H1010","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S318-2004?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Coins","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S318-2004","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1010-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denominations and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 10th June 2004 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1010-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE CHINA-SINGAPORE SUZHOU INDUSTRIAL PARK 10TH ANNIVERSARY COMMEMORATIVE COIN\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandard Weight\nStandard Diameter\nStandard Thickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n10 Dollars\n999.9 Fine Gold\n31.1035 g +\n0.05 g\n32.12 mm +\n0.15 mm\n2.50 mm +\n0.20 mm\nRound\nMilled and bearing the words “MONETARY AUTHORITY OF SINGAPORE” and lion symbol.\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the yeardate “2004” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin. The position of the mintmark is at 5 o’clock for the $10 gold and $10 silver coins.\nThe main feature of the coin is the “Harmony” sculpture at the China-Singapore Suzhou Industrial Park (CS-SIP) that was jointly unveiled by SM Lee Kuan Yew and China’s Vice-Premier Li Lanqing in 2001 to symbolise the close bilateral relations. The “Harmony” sculpture resembling the figure “8”, symbolises “prosperity” and “abundance” for CS-SIP and its tenants.\n10 Dollars\n999 Fine Silver\n20.00 g + 0.20 g\n38.70 mm + 0.15 mm\n2.50 mm + 0.30 mm\nRound\n10 Dollars\n75% Copper 25% Nickel\n28.00 g + 0.40 g\n40.70 mm + 0.15 mm\n3.05 mm + 0.20 mm\nRound\nThe words “CHINASINGAPORE SUZHOU INDUSTRIAL PARK” in English and Chinese, above and below the sculpture respectively, signify the central theme of the coin.\nThe ’$10’ denomination to the left of the sculpture and the words “10th Anniversary” in English and Chinese to the right provide the overall balance to the design.\n(a) Common Reverse Design for $10 Gold Proof Coin, $10 Silver Proof Coin and $10 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin.\n(b) Common Obverse Design for $10 Gold Proof Coin and $10 Silver Proof Coin.\n(c) Obverse Design of $10 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Coins","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1011"] = {"id":"SG-H1011","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S800-2005?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Coins","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S800-2005","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1011-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nCurrency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Coins\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denomination and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 27th December 2005 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1011-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE 2005 SINGAPORE IDENTITY PLAN (URBAN VILLAGES) COINS TO BE ISSUED ON 27TH DECEMBER 2005\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandard Weight\nStandard\nDiameter\nStandard\nThickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n1 Dollar\n999 Fine Silver\n17.30 g + 0.20 g\n33.00 + mm 0.15 mm\n2.45 mm + 0.30 mm\nRound\nMilled\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2005” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin. The position of the mintmark is at 5 o’clock of the coin.\nUrban Villages refer to places in Anak Bukit, Coronation, Jalan Leban and Casuarina Road, Springleaf and Thomson Village. Located along Bukit Timah Road and Thomson Road, 2 of the oldest roads from the city centre, these activity nodes have evolved into local village centres. Their close location to the Central Water Catchment, coupled with their low-rise, small-scale, “village-like” qualities give them a distinct character.\nAnak Bukit\nAlso known as the 7th milestone of Bukit Timah Road, Anak Bukit area today has a variety of eating houses and retail shops. Depicted on the coin is a diverse stretch of eateries with their distinctive outdoor forecourts and the Bukit Timah Hill in the background with the 2 prominent radio towers.\nCoronation\nIn recent years, Coronation has added another dimension to its charming character with the arrival of numerous food and beverages outlets serving fine food in quietly-chic environments. Depicted on the coin is Cluny Court, a row of 2-storey shophouses that include wine stores, specialist delis and fanciful food and beverages outlets catering to the up-market lifestyle.\nJalan Leban and Casuarina Road\nLocated just next to the Central Water Catchment, the Jalan Leban and Casuarina area is set in quiet and green surroundings. The coin features the shophouses at Jalan Leban that were built more than 40 years ago as a local shopping node. Adding a touch of distinctiveness to this area is the one-storey red-roofed taxi service station.\nSpringleaf\nSpringleaf is an attractive natural wooded area tucked away in lush green settings. The former Mandai Post Office at Mandai Road/Upper Thomson Road and the wooden bridge along Nee Soon Road featured on the coin are distinctive landmarks that give the Springleaf area its special identity.\nThomson Village\nThomson Village consists of a stretch of 2-storey shophouses on both sides of Upper Thomson Road, near Sin Ming Road junction. Depicted on the coin is this row of shophouses that were built in the 1960s. Today, it is a vibrant area, thriving with interesting mix of shops, eating places, hair salons, pet shops and clinics.\nThe denomination of the coin in figures is to the left of the main design on all the coins.\n1 Dollar\n75% Copper\n25% Nickel\n18.00 g 0.30 g\n33.00 mm 0.15 mm\n2.80 mm 0.30 mm\nRound\nMilled\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2005” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin.\n(a) Reverse Design of the $1 Silver Proof Coin (Anak Bukit).\n(b) Reverse Design of the $1 Silver Proof Coin (Coronation).\n(c) Reverse Design of the $1 Silver Proof Coin (Jalan Leban and Casuarina Road).\n(d) Reverse Design of the $1 Silver Proof Coin (Springleaf).\n(e) Reverse Design of the $1 Silver Proof Coin (Thomson Village).\n(f) Reverse Design of the $1 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin (Anak Bukit).\n(g) Reverse Design of the $1 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin (Coronation).\n(h) Reverse Design of the $1 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin (Jalan Leban and Casuarina Road).\n(i) Reverse Design of the $1 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin (Springleaf).\n(j) Reverse Design of the $1 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin (Thomson Village).\n(k) Common Obverse Design for $1 Silver Proof Coins (Anak Bukit, Coronation, Jalan Leban and Casuarina Road, Springleaf and Thomson Village).\n(l) Common Obverse Design for $1 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coins (Anak Bukit, Coronation, Jalan Leban and Casuarina Road, Springleaf and Thomson Village)."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Coins","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1012"] = {"id":"SG-H1012","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S119-2002?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——纸币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Currency Note","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S119-2002","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1012-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denomination and characteristics of the currency note to be issued on 15th March 2002 by the Board of Commissioners of Currency, Singapore, are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1012-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDenomination\nSize\nDesign\nPredominant and Secondary Colours\n$50\nLength – 156mm\nWidth – 74mm\nFront\nBack\nFront\nBack\nThe front design features the portrait of Singapore’s first President, the late Encik Yusof bin Ishak. The word “SINGAPORE” is printed on the bottom centre portion. The Singapore Arms is positioned on the upper left-hand corner while the denomination numeral “50” is located at the upper middle and bottom right-hand corner. The silver coloured octagonal kinegram showing colourful images of the denomination numeral “50”, BCCS logo and the letters “BCCS” is located above the words “THIS NOTE IS LEGAL TENDER”. The signature and seal of Mr Lee Hsien Loong, Chairman, Board of Commissioners of Currency, Singapore, are below the words “FIFTY DOLLARS”. The “Lift Twin”, bearing the latent image of the BCCS logo and the Singapore Lion symbol, is at the lower centre of the note. A “Singapore Lion” is located at the left-hand side of the word “SINGAPORE”. One set of the asymmetrical serial number appears horizontal on the left-hand side while the other set appears vertical on the right-hand side.\nThe back design features 4 ethnic musical instruments, the Chinese Pipa, Malay Kompang, Indian Veena and Classical Violin, as well as 2 local artworks entitled “Gibbons” and “Drying Salted Fish” respectively. The word “SINGAPORE” is located at the top centre portion of the note. The “Singapore Lion” is to the lower left of the Portrait watermark and is in perfect registration with that on the front. The denomination numeral “50” is located at the top left-hand corner and to the left of the “Singapore Lion”. The word “ARTS” is located below the artwork “Drying Salted Fish”.\nPredominant\nColour: Blue\nSecondary Colours:\nDark Blue and Red\nPredominant\nColour: Blue\nSecondary Colours:\nDark Blue and Red\nThe note has one windowed machine-readable thread which is interwoven in the paper running vertically down the back of the note. A watermark of the President’s portrait is incorporated on the left-hand side of the front of the note. The watermark is not overprinted on both the front and back. A highlight watermark which corresponds with the Braille pattern at the top right-hand corner of the front design, is located at the bottom of the Portrait watermark."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Currency Note","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1013"] = {"id":"SG-H1013","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S210-2007?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——纸币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Currency Note","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S210-2007","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1013-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denomination and characteristics of the currency note to be issued on 18th May 2007 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1013-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDenomination\nSize\nDesign\nPredominant and Secondary Colours\nFront\nBack\nFront\nBack\n$5\nLength — 133mm\nWidth– 66mm\nThe front design features the portrait of Singapore’s first President, the late Encik Yusof bin Ishak. The word “SINGAPORE” is printed on the bottom centre portion. There are 2 see-through windows — one on the upper left-hand corner and the other at the bottom right-hand corner. The complex clear window on the upper left-hand corner has a multi-tonal Singapore Arms. The clear window at the bottom right-hand corner is embossed with the numeral “5” in its centre, with repetitive pattern of the numeral “5” at the top and bottom of the window. The shadow image of the President’s portrait is on the left-hand side of the note. The denomination numeral “5” is located at the upper middle portion. The Singapore Lion symbol in gold patch with the words “FIVE DOLLARS” and the image of the Singapore Arms is located above the words “THIS NOTE IS LEGAL TENDER”. The signature and seal of Mr Goh Chok Tong, Chairman, Monetary Authority of Singapore, are below the words “FIVE DOLLARS”. The security thread in the shape of Singapore island is at the lower centre of the note. The Singapore Lion symbol is located at the left-hand side of the word “SINGAPORE”. One set of the asymmetrical serial number appears horizontal on the left-hand side while the other set appears vertical on the right-hand side. The Braille pattern is at the top right-hand corner.\nThe back design features a Garden City landscape dominated by the portrayal of the old tembusu tree (Fagraea fragrans). In the foreground of the design lies a bed of Singapore’s national flower, the Vanda Miss Joaquim. The word “SINGAPORE” is located at the top centre portion of the note. The Singapore Lion symbol is at the lower left of the shadow image of the President’s portrait and is in perfect registration with that on the front. The denomination numeral “5” is located at the top left-hand corner and to the left of the Singapore Lion symbol. The words “GARDEN CITY” are located below the picture of the tembusu tree. The 2 see-through windows located at the front of the note can also be seen from the back.\nPredominant Colour: Green\nSecondary Colours: Dark Green and Red\nPredominant Colour: Green\nSecondary Colours: Dark Green and Red\n(a) Front Design of the $5 Polymer Portrait Note.\n(b) Back Design of the $5 Polymer Portrait Note.\nActual size: 133 mm x 66 mm"}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Currency Note","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1014"] = {"id":"SG-H1014","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S822-2005?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——纸币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Currency Note","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S822-2005","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1014-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denomination and characteristics of the currency note to be issued on 22nd December 2005 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1014-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDenomination\nSize\nDesign\nPredominant and Secondary Colours\nFront\nBack\nFront\nBack\n$2\nLength – 126mm\nWidth– 63mm\nThe front design features the portrait of Singapore’s first President, the late Encik Yusof bin Ishak. The word “SINGAPORE” is printed on the bottom centre portion. There are 2 see-through windows – one on the upper left-hand corner and the other at the bottom right-hand corner. The complex clear window on the upper left-hand corner has a multi-tonal Singapore Arms. The clear window at the bottom right-hand corner is embossed with the numeral “2” in its centre, with repetitive pattern of the numeral “2” at the top and bottom of the window. The shadow image of the President’s portrait is on the left-hand side of the note. The denomination numeral “2” is located at the upper middle portion.\nThe back design features a group of pupils interacting with a teacher in a “Borderless” classroom. Three prominent institutions of learning are seen in the background below the word “SINGAPORE”, printed on the top centre portion of the note. The Singapore Lion symbol is at the lower left of the shadow image of the President’s portrait and is in perfect registration with that on the front. The denomination numeral “2” is located at the top left-hand corner and to the left of the Singapore Lion symbol. The word “EDUCATION” is located at the bottom centre of the note. The 2 see-through windows located at the front of the note can also be seen from the back.\nPredominant Colour: Purple\nSecondary Colours: Blue and Brown\nPredominant Colour: Purple\nSecondary Colours: Blue and Brown\nThe Singapore Lion symbol in gold patch with the words “TWO DOLLARS” and the image of the Singapore Arms is located above the words “THIS NOTE IS LEGAL TENDER”. The signature and seal of Mr Goh Chok Tong, Chairman, Monetary Authority of Singapore, are below the words “TWO DOLLARS”. The security thread in the shape of Singapore island is at the lower centre of the note. The Singapore Lion symbol is located at the left-hand side of the word “SINGAPORE”. One set of the asymmetrical serial number appears horizontal on the left-hand side while the other set appears vertical on the right-hand side. The Braille pattern is at the top right-hand corner.\n(a) Front Design of the $2 Polymer Portrait Note.\n(b) Back Design of the $2 Polymer Portrait Note.\nActual size: 126 mm x 63 mm"}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denomination and Characteristics of Currency Note","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1015"] = {"id":"SG-H1015","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S350-2008?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S350-2008","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1015-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denominations and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 8th July 2008 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1015-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE 2008 SINGAPORE 43 YEARS OF INDEPENDENCE COMMEMORATIVE COINS TO BE ISSUED ON 8TH JULY 2008\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandard Weight\nStandard Diameter\nStandard Thickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n2 Dollars\n999 Fine Silver\n20.00 g + 0.20 g\n38.70 mm + 0.15 mm\n2.50 mm + 0.30 mm\nRound\nMilled and bearing the words “MONETARY AUTHORITY OF SINGAPORE” and lion symbol.\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2008” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin. The position of the mintmark is at 5 o’clock of the coin.\nThe official 2008 National Day Parade (NDP) logo, “Celebrating the Singapore Spirit”, serves as the central feature of the coin design. The logo comprises 5 young Singaporeans, reaching out for the stars — striving for excellence, determined to succeed and confident of a brighter future ahead.\nAlso depicted on the coin is the newly inaugurated Singapore Flyer that symbolises Singapore as a global city and in the background, various NDP activities associated with the floating platform on Marina Bay venue.\nThe denomination is at the left of the coin.\n2 Dollars\n75% Copper\n25% Nickel\n20.00 g + 0.60 g\n38.70 mm + 0.15 mm\n2.50 mm + 0.30 mm\nRound\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2008” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin.\n(a) Reverse Design of the $2 Silver Proof Coin.\n(b) Reverse Design of the $2 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin.\n(c) Obverse Design for $2 Silver Proof Coin.\n(d) Obverse Design for $2 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1016"] = {"id":"SG-H1016","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S423-2001?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S423-2001","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1016-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denominations and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 5th September 2001 by the Board of Commissioners of Currency, Singapore, are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1016-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE 20 YEARS OF PRODUCTIVITY MOVEMENT IN SINGAPORE $5 COMMEMORATIVE COINS\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandard Weight\nStandard Diameter\nStandard Thickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n5 Dollars\n92.5% Silver\n7.5% Copper\n20.00 g\n+ 0.20 g\n38.70 mm\n+ 0.15 mm\n2.45 mm\n+ 0.20 mm\nRound\nMilled and bearing the words “BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS OF CURRENCY, SINGAPORE” and the lion symbol.\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2001” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin. The position of the mintmark is at 5 o’clock of the coin.\nThe design of the coin depicts the winds of change that have shaped the Singapore economy in the last 20 years since the launch of the Productivity Movement in 1981.\nTwenty years ago, the Movement emphasised Quality to support Singapore’s investment-driven phase of development. Today, Innovation, together with Quality is emphasised to support the innovation-driven phase of development. This progression is symbolized by the bold and vibrant strokes rising from the base of the coin in a dynamic upward movement.\n5 Dollars\n75% Copper\n25% Nickel\n20.00 g\n+ 0.40 g\n38.70 mm\n+ 0.125 mm\n2.45 mm\n+ 0.152 mm\nRound\nMilled and bearing the words “BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS OF CURRENCY, SINGAPORE” and the lion symbol.\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2001” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin.\nThe year 1981 marks the first lap of the Movement. The progress made since then is accentuated by the two sets of arrowheads that link the words “Quality” and “Innovation”. The year 2001 signals the start of the new lap for the Movement.\nThe difference made by the Movement in the last 20 years is strongly underlined by the words “PRODUCTIVITY MOVEMENT” bordering the coin. The four sparks radiating from these words represent the four major outcomes that are evident today:\nA world-class workforce\nA world-class industry\nA world-class infrastructure\nA world-class people & lifestyle.\n(a) Reverse design of $5 Sterling Silver Proof and $5 Uncirculated Cupro-nickel coins.\n(b) Obverse design of $5 Sterling Silver Proof coin.\n(c) Obverse design of $5 Uncirculated Cupro-nickel coin."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1017"] = {"id":"SG-H1017","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S450-2008?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S450-2008","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1017-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denominations and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 12th September 2008 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1017-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE 2008 FORMULA 1™ SINGTEL SINGAPORE GRAND PRIX COMMEMORATIVE COINS TO BE ISSUED ON 12TH SEPTEMBER 2008\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandard Weight\nStandard Diameter\nStandard Thickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n100 Dollars\n999.9\nFine Gold\n7.776 g +\n0.05 g\n21.96 mm + 0.125 mm\n1.55 mm + 0.20 mm\nRound\nMilled\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2008” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin. The position of the mintmark is at 5 o’clock for all the proof coins.\nThe design captures the spectacular mood of dazzling lights and vibrancy of the night race and landmarks in the Marina area that forms part of the street circuit. Also featured on the coin are the Formula 1™ SingTel Singapore Grand Prix Logo and the 2 most recognisable icons in auto racing namely, a F1 car in full throttle and the winning chequered flag.\nThe denomination is at the top-right of the coin.\n50 Dollars\n999\nFine Silver\n20.00 g + 0.20 g\n38.70 mm + 0.15 mm\n2.50 mm + 0.30 mm\nRound\nMilled and bearing the words “MONETARY AUTHORITY OF SINGAPORE” and lion symbol.\n2 Dollars\n75%\nCopper\n25%\nNickel\n20.00 g + 0.60 g\n38.70 mm + 0.15 mm\n2.50 mm + 0.30 mm\nRound\n(a) Reverse Design of the $100 Gold Proof Coin.\n(b) Reverse Design of the $50 Silver Proof Coin.\n(c) Reverse Design of the $2 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin.\n(d) Obverse Design for $100 Gold Proof Coin.\n(e) Obverse Design for $50 Silver Proof Coin.\n(f) Obverse Design for $2 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1018"] = {"id":"SG-H1018","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S534-2008?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S534-2008","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1018-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denominations and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 28th October 2008 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1018-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE 2008 HERITAGE ORCHIDS OF SINGAPORE COINS TO BE ISSUED ON 28TH OCTOBER 2008\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandard Weight\nStandard Diameter\nStandard Thickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n5 Dollars\n999 Fine Silver\n20.00 g + 0.20 g\n38.70 mm + 0.15 mm\n2.50 mm + 0.30 mm\nRound\nMilled and bearing the words “MONETARY AUTHORITY OF SINGAPORE” and lion symbol.\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2008” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin. The position of the mintmark is at 5 o’clock of the coin.\nThe coin design features the orchid “Oncidium Goldiana” in full colour in the lower-right to upper-left of the coin and the name of the orchid is along the upper circumference. The weight in grams and fineness of the coin are at the lower circumference. The denomination is at the left of the coin.\n5 Dollars\n999 Fine Silver\n20.00 g + 0.20 g\n38.70 mm + 0.15 mm\n2.50 mm + 0.30 mm\nRound\nThe coin design features the orchid “Aranda Tay Swee Eng” in full colour in the lower centre of the coin and the name of the orchid is along the upper circumference. The weight in grams and fineness of the coin are at the lower circumference. The denomination is at the right of the coin.\n(a) Reverse Design of the $5 Silver Proof Coin (Oncidium Goldiana).\n(b) Reverse Design of the $5 Silver Proof Coin (Aranda Tay Swee Eng).\n(c) Common Obverse Design for $5 Silver Proof Coins (Oncidium Goldiana and Aranda Tay Swee Eng)."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1019"] = {"id":"SG-H1019","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S611-2009?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S611-2009","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1019-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denominations and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 1st January 2010 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1019-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF COINS IN THE 2010 UNCIRCULATED COIN SET TO BE ISSUED ON 1ST JANUARY 2010\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandard Weight\nStandard Diameter\nStandard Thickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n5 Cents\nCopper 92%\nAluminium 6%\nNickel 2%\n1.56 g + 0.062 g\n16.75 + mm\n0.125 mm\n1.22 + mm\n0.152 mm\nRound\nMilled\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2010” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coins. A ring of dashes surrounds the Singapore Arms on both the 10-cent and 50-cent coins. The $1 coin has an octagonal frame along the circumference. The $5 coin has a ring of beads surrounding the Singapore Arms with a latent image below it depicting the year-date “2010” and MAS logo.\nThe Fruit Salad Plant (Monstera deliciosa) in the centre with the figure “5” below it and the words “FIVE CENTS” above it.\n10 Cents\nCopper 75%\nNickel 25%\n2.60 g + 0.065 g\n18.50 mm + 0.125 mm\n1.38 mm + 0.152 mm\nRound\nMilled\nThe Star Jasmine (Jasminum multiflorum) in the centre with the figure “10” below it and the words “TEN CENTS” above it.\n20 Cents\nCopper 75%\nNickel 25%\n4.50 g + 0.112 g\n21.36 mm + 0.125 mm\n1.72 mm + 0.152 mm\nRound\nMilled\nThe Powder-puff Plant (Calliandra surinamensis) in the centre with the figure “20” below it and the words “TWENTY CENTS” above it.\n50 Cents\nCopper 75%\nNickel 25%\n7.29 g + 0.145 g\n24.66 mm + 0.125 mm\n2.06 mm + 0.152 mm\nRound\nPlain and bearing the words “REPUBLIC OF SINGAPORE” and lion symbol.\nThe yellow Allamanda (Allamanda cathartica) in the centre with the figure “50” below it and the words “FIFTY CENTS” above it.\n1 Dollar\nCopper 92%\nAluminium 6%\nNickel 2%\n6.30 g + 0.13 g\n22.40 mm + 0.125 mm\n2.40 mm + 0.152 mm\nRound\nMilled and bearing the words “REPUBLIC OF SINGAPORE” and lion symbol.\nThe Periwinkle (Lochnera rosea) in the centre with 4 smaller periwinkles surrounding it, the figure “$1” below it and the words “ONE DOLLAR” above it. An octagonal frame surrounds the design.\n5 Dollars\nOuter Ring —\n6.70 g + 0.17 g\n23.30 mm + 0.125 mm\n2.30 mm + 0.152 mm\nScallop\nPlain\nThe flower, Vanda Miss Joaquim in the centre with the figure “$5” below it. A ring of beads surrounds the flower with the words “FIVE DOLLARS” and 5 dots at the outer ring of the coin.\nCopper 75%\nNickel 25%\nCore —\nCopper 92%\nAluminium 6%\nNickel 2%\n(a) Reverse Design of the 5-cent Base Metal Coin.\n(b) Reverse Design of the 10-cent Base Metal Coin.\n(c) Reverse Design of the 20-cent Base Metal Coin.\n(d) Reverse Design of the 50-cent Base Metal Coin.\n(e) Reverse Design of the $1 Base Metal Coin.\n(f) Reverse Design of the $5 Base Metal Coin.\n(g) Obverse Design for 5-cent Base Metal Coin.\n(h) Obverse Design for 10-cent Base Metal Coin.\n(i) Obverse Design for 20-cent Base Metal Coin.\n(j) Obverse Design for 50-cent Base Metal Coin.\n(k) Obverse Design for $1 Base Metal Coin.\n(l) Obverse Design for $5 Base Metal Coin.\n(m) Latent Image."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
  FT["SG-H1020"] = {"id":"SG-H1020","coverage":"full","language":"en","sourceName":"Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore Statutes Online (SSO)","sourceUrl":"https://sso.agc.gov.sg/SL/CA1967-S612-2009?ViewType=Print&PrintType=html&ProvIds=all-.","fetchedAt":"2026-10-07","articleCount":2,"structureMode":"WHOLE","title":{"zh":"货币法——硬币面额与特征","en":"Currency Act — Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","orig":null},"level":"regulation","status":"active","docNumber":"Subsidiary Legislation CA1967-S612-2009","completenessNote":"该法规为单篇法律文书，无分节结构；正文按官方 front 抬头逐字全录，共 2 条。","country":"SG","countryName":"新加坡","layer":"intl","articles":[{"id":"SG-H1020-A1","no":1,"noLabel":{"en":"1"},"text":{"en":"Currency Act\n(Chapter 69)\nPursuant to section 17(5) of the Currency Act, it is hereby notified for general information that the denominations and characteristics of the coins to be issued on 1st January 2010 by the Monetary Authority of Singapore are as shown in the Schedule."}},{"id":"SG-H1020-A900000","no":900000,"noLabel":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE"},"text":{"en":"THE SCHEDULE\nDESCRIPTION OF THE 2010 CHINESE ALMANAC COINS (YEAR OF THE TIGER) TO BE ISSUED ON 1st January 2010\nDenomination\nMetal\nStandard Weight\nStandard Diameter\nStandard Thickness\nShape\nEdge\nDesign of Obverse\nDesign of Reverse\n200 Dollars\n999.9\nFine Gold\n155.52 g + 0.20 g\n60.00 mm + 0.30 mm\n3.80 mm + 0.30 mm\nRound\nMilled and bearing the words “MONETARY AUTHORITY OF SINGAPORE” and lion symbol.\nThe Singapore Arms in the centre with the year-date “2010” below it and the word “SINGAPORE” in the 4 official languages around the circumference of the coin. The position of the mintmark is at 5 o’clock for all the proof coins.\nInspired by Chinese calligraphy, the tiger in the centre is symbolised by the appropriate Chinese character of the “Heavenly Stems and Earthly Branches” to enhance the characteristics of the animal and the oriental cultural flavour. The peony, a traditional Chinese symbol of blessings and festive cheer, incorporated on the body of the tiger, ushers in the Lunar New Year. The 2 Chinese characters signifying the “Year of the Tiger” are below the design. The denomination of the coin in figures is above the design. The weight in troy oz/grams and fineness of the coin are at the lower circumference for all the coins except for the $2 cupro-nickel coin.\n100 Dollars\n999.9\nFine Gold\n31.103 g + 0.05 g\n33.00 mm + 0.15 mm\n2.45 mm + 0.20 mm\nRound\n25 Dollars\n999\nFine Silver\n155.52 g + 0.90 g\n65.00 mm + 0.30 mm\n5.40 mm + 0.30 mm\nRound\n10 Dollars\n999\nFine Silver\n62.206 g + 0.20 g\n45.00 mm + 0.15 mm\n4.40 mm + 0.20 mm\nRound\n5 Dollars\n999.9\nFine Gold\n7.776 g +\n0.05 g\n21.96 mm + 0.125 mm\n1.55 mm + 0.20 mm\nRound\nMilled\n2 Dollars\n999\nFine Silver\n20.00 g\n0.20 g\n38.70 mm + 0.15 mm\n2.50 mm + 0.30 mm\nRound\nMilled and bearing the words “MONETARY AUTHORITY OF SINGAPORE” and lion symbol.\n2 Dollars\n75%\nCopper\n25%\nNickel\n20.00 g\n0.60 g\n38.70 mm + 0.15 mm\n2.50 mm + 0.30 mm\nRound\n1 Dollar\n999.9\nFine Gold\n0.30 g +\n0.04 g\n7.00 mm + 0.15 mm\n0.70 mm + 0.15 mm\nRound\nPlain\n(a) Reverse Design of the $200 Gold Proof Coin.\n(b) Reverse Design of the $100 Gold Proof Coin.\n(c) Reverse Design of the $5 Gold Proof Coin.\n(d) Reverse Design of the $1 Gold (Brilliant Uncirculated) Coin.\n(e) Reverse Design of the $25 Silver Proof Coin.\n(f) Reverse Design of the $10 Silver Piedfort Proof Coin.\n(g) Reverse Design of the $2 Silver Proof Coin.\n(h) Reverse Design of the $2 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin.\n(i) Common Obverse Design for $200 Gold Proof Coin, $100 Gold Proof Coin and $5 Gold Proof Coin.\n(j) Obverse Design for $1 Gold (Brilliant Uncirculated) Coin.\n(k) Common Obverse Design for $25 Silver Proof Coin, $10 Silver Piedfort Proof Coin and $2 Silver Proof Coin.\n(l) Obverse Design for $2 Cupro-Nickel Proof-Like Coin."}}],"pack":"sg-p13","titleTranslation":{"engine":"llm","model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewed":false,"batch":"CALR-TITLE-20261017-1818-SG-2467","source":"Currency Act — Denominations and Characteristics of Coins","note":"由翻译 agent 按《法规标题中译规范》产出，经 tools/verify-title-return.js 机器校验后入库；尚未人工复核。"}};
